{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01G4NdKywMmMviGS6K8ZzD3h","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 1. Checkout Flow & Form UX\n2\t\n3\tInterview-depth answers for payment system frontend design: problem framing, approach, tradeoffs, and concrete implementation details. Section covers multi-step flow architecture, card input UX, validation strategy, autocomplete, loading states, and progressive disclosure.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Core\n8\t\n9\t### Walk me through how you would architect a multi-step checkout flow (cart → shipping → payment → confirmation). Where does state live, how do you handle back-navigation, and how do you prevent users from skipping steps?\n10\t\n11\t**Problem framing:** A multi-step checkout is a linear wizard with real monetary consequences — partial state must survive navigation, back-nav must not lose data, and skipping steps can produce incomplete orders or security holes (e.g. confirming payment before collecting shipping address). Getting this wrong directly costs revenue.\n12\t\n13\t**Approach:**\n14\t\n15\tModel the checkout as a **state machine** with four nodes and explicit transition guards:\n16\t\n17\t```mermaid\n18\tstateDiagram-v2\n19\t    [*] --> Cart\n20\t    Cart --> Shipping : cartValid\n21\t    Shipping --> Payment : shippingValid\n22\t    Payment --> Confirmation : paymentSucceeded\n23\t    Payment --> Payment : paymentFailed (retry)\n24\t    Confirmation --> [*]\n25\t```\n26\t\n27\t**State residence:**\n28\t\n29\t| Layer | What lives there |\n30\t|-------|-----------------|\n31\t| URL / router param | Current step (`/checkout/shipping`) — makes back-nav free |\n32\t| React context / Zustand store | `cartItems`, `shippingAddress`, `selectedMethod` (non-sensitive) |\n33\t| Server-side session | `cartId`, `orderId`, `paymentIntentId` — authoritative |\n34\t| Never persisted | Raw card numbers, CVV — only ever in Stripe's iframe |\n35\t\n36\t**Step gating:** Each route renders a `StepGuard` higher-order component. Before rendering step N, it checks that all steps 1…N-1 have valid data in the store. If not, it redirects to the earliest incomplete step. This prevents URL manipulation to skip steps.\n37\t\n38\t```\n39\t/checkout/payment → StepGuard checks: hasCart && hasValidShipping\n40\t  → if false: redirect /checkout/shipping\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\t**Back navigation:** Because step state lives in the store (not component state), pressing the browser back button to `/checkout/shipping` restores all previously entered values instantly. The store is seeded from `sessionStorage` on mount so a refresh doesn't lose work:\n44\t\n45\t```ts\n46\t// On store init\n47\tconst persisted = sessionStorage.getItem('checkout_draft')\n48\tif (persisted) store.hydrate(JSON.parse(persisted))\n49\t\n50\t// On every store mutation\n51\tstore.subscribe(state => sessionStorage.setItem('checkout_draft', JSON.stringify(state)))\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t**Confirmation is server-authoritative:** The confirmation page never trusts client state alone. It fetches `GET /orders/:id` on mount and reconciles against what the client believes. This catches redirect failures, query-param stripping, and race conditions.\n55\t\n56\t**Tradeoffs:** Storing step state in the URL (query params per field) is too verbose and leaks data in logs. Storing purely in component state loses data on back-nav. The store + sessionStorage hybrid gives the best UX at the cost of needing a clear/invalidate mechanism when the user abandons (handle via `beforeunload` → `DELETE /carts/:id/draft` and `sessionStorage.clear()`).\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t### How do you design a credit card input form that provides real-time feedback — card type detection, formatted input masking (e.g., `4242 4242 4242 4242`), expiry validation, and CVV length rules — without blocking the user while they type?\n61\t\n62\t**Problem framing:** Card input is the highest-friction moment in checkout. Unformatted numbers are error-prone, wrong CVV length expectations cause failures, and aggressive validation frustrates users. All formatting logic must run synchronously inside the keypress handler or it feels laggy.\n63\t\n64\t**Approach:** In practice, **always use Stripe Elements or a hosted fields solution** — raw card input on your page puts you in PCI SAQ D scope. But the UX logic is worth understanding for non-Stripe contexts or custom implementations on top of a vault tokenization service.\n65\t\n66\t**Card type detection** from the first 1–4 digits (BIN range):\n67\t\n68\t```ts\n69\tconst CARD_PATTERNS: [RegExp, CardType][] = [\n70\t  [/^4/, 'visa'],                    // Visa: starts with 4\n71\t  [/^5[1-5]|^2[2-7]/, 'mastercard'],// MC: 51-55 or 2221-2720\n72\t  [/^3[47]/, 'amex'],               // Amex: 34 or 37\n73\t  [/^6(?:011|5)/, 'discover'],\n74\t]\n75\t\n76\tfunction detectCard(value: string): CardType {\n77\t  const digits = value.replace(/\\D/g, '')\n78\t  return CARD_PATTERNS.find(([re]) => re.test(digits))?.[1] ?? 'unknown'\n79\t}\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tDetect on every keystroke but only *display* the card brand icon after 2+ digits to avoid flicker.\n83\t\n84\t**Formatting mask:** On `input` event, strip non-digits, then re-insert spaces at the right positions. Use `selectionStart` to maintain cursor position through the mask:\n85\t\n86\t```ts\n87\tfunction formatCard(raw: string, type: CardType): string {\n88\t  const digits = raw.replace(/\\D/g, '').slice(0, type === 'amex' ? 15 : 16)\n89\t  if (type === 'amex') {\n90\t    // 4-6-5 pattern: 3782 822463 10005\n91\t    return digits.replace(/^(\\d{4})(\\d{0,6})(\\d{0,5})/, (_, a, b, c) =>\n92\t      [a, b, c].filter(Boolean).join(' '))\n93\t  }\n94\t  // 4-4-4-4 pattern\n95\t  return digits.match(/.{1,4}/g)?.join(' ') ?? digits\n96\t}\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\t**Expiry validation:**\n100\t- Accept `MM/YY` or `MMYY` — auto-insert `/` after month digits\n101\t- Validate: month 01–12, year >= current year\n102\t- If month is current month and year is current year: still valid (expires end of month)\n103\t\n104\t**CVV length:**\n105\t- Visa/MC/Discover: 3 digits\n106\t- Amex: 4 digits\n107\t- Enforce `maxLength` attribute dynamically based on detected card type\n108\t\n109\t**Non-blocking:** All of the above runs synchronously in the event handler — it's pure string manipulation, O(16) worst case. No async operations in the hot path. The card icon swap (image/SVG) is instant since all card brand assets are preloaded.\n110\t\n111\t**Tradeoffs:** Hand-rolling this is ~300 lines of edge cases (paste handling, mobile keyboard quirks, screen reader announcements for format changes). Libraries like `cleave.js` or `card-validator` handle most of this. More importantly, doing any of this on your own page means raw PANs enter your JS environment — default to Stripe Elements instead.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t### What is the correct strategy for inline vs. summary error display in a payment form? When should you validate on `blur` vs. `change` vs. `submit`, and how do you avoid frustrating users with premature errors on partially-typed card numbers?\n116\t\n117\t**Problem framing:** Validation timing is one of the highest-impact UX details in checkout forms. Too eager (validate on every keypress) → errors appear while the user is mid-typing, feel accusatory. Too late (validate only on submit) → the user fills 4 fields wrong and sees a wall of errors at once.\n118\t\n119\t**Approach — three-phase validation:**\n120\t\n121\t| Phase | Trigger | What runs |\n122\t|-------|---------|-----------|\n123\t| **Lenient** | `change` (keypress) | Format masking only, no error display |\n124\t| **Intermediate** | `blur` (field exit) | Full field validation, show error if invalid |\n125\t| **Final** | `submit` | Validate all fields, prevent submit, scroll to first error |\n126\t\n127\t**The key rule:** Never show a validation error on a field the user has not yet interacted with. Track `touched` state per field:\n128\t\n129\t```ts\n130\tconst [touched, setTouched] = useState<Set<string>>(new Set())\n131\t\n132\tconst handleBlur = (field: string) => {\n133\t  setTouched(prev => new Set(prev).add(field))\n134\t}\n135\t\n136\t// Only show error if field has been touched\n137\tconst showError = (field: string) => touched.has(field) && !!errors[field]\n138\t```\n139\t\n140\t**Card number special case:** A 16-digit Visa number is partially valid at digits 1–15. Don't validate until: (a) the field loses focus (`blur`), OR (b) the user has typed the maximum length for the detected card type. This prevents the \"Your card number is invalid\" error appearing after the 5th digit.\n141\t\n142\t```ts\n143\tconst isCardComplete = (value: string, type: CardType) => {\n144\t  const digits = value.replace(/\\D/g, '')\n145\t  return digits.length === (type === 'amex' ? 15 : 16)\n146\t}\n147\t\n148\t// Show error only if complete OR blurred\n149\tif (isCardComplete(value, cardType) || touched.has('cardNumber')) {\n150\t  // run luhn check\n151\t}\n152\t```\n153\t\n154\t**Inline vs. summary display:**\n155\t- **Inline** (below each field): Best for field-level errors — user knows exactly which field to fix\n156\t- **Summary** (top of form or near submit): Use for cross-field errors (e.g. \"Billing ZIP doesn't match card\") or server errors that span multiple fields\n157\t- **Never both** for the same error — it feels redundant and confusing\n158\t\n159\t**After submit failure:** Re-validate all fields immediately, mark all as `touched`, focus the first error field, announce the error count to screen readers via `aria-live`.\n160\t\n161\t**Tradeoffs:** The `touched` + `blur` pattern is standard (Formik, React Hook Form both default to this). The main alternative — showing errors only on submit — has higher error density at the worst moment and is favored only when form length is ≤ 2 fields. The eager `onChange` validation pattern is appropriate only for async checks like coupon code validation where the user explicitly wants live feedback.\n162\t\n163\t---\n164\t\n165\t### How do you handle address autocomplete (Google Places API, Smarty Streets) in a checkout form — including debouncing, fallback to manual entry when the API is unavailable, and ensuring the autofilled address is still validated server-side?\n166\t\n167\t**Problem framing:** Address autocomplete reduces typing effort and typo-induced failed deliveries, but introduces risks: API failures silently break the field, autofilled addresses bypass field-level validation, and users in rural areas or new buildings get no suggestions. The integration must degrade gracefully.\n168\t\n169\t**Approach:**\n170\t\n171\t**Debounced input → autocomplete fetch:**\n172\t\n173\t```ts\n174\tconst debouncedFetch = useMemo(\n175\t  () => debounce(async (query: string) => {\n176\t    if (query.length < 3) { setSuggestions([]); return }\n177\t    try {\n178\t      const results = await placesAutocomplete(query, { country: 'US' })\n179\t      setSuggestions(results)\n180\t    } catch {\n181\t      // Fail silently — fallback to manual entry\n182\t      setSuggestions([])\n183\t    }\n184\t  }, 300),\n185\t  []\n186\t)\n187\t```\n188\t\n189\t300ms debounce: aggressive enough to feel fast, conservative enough to avoid per-keystroke API calls (Google Places charges per request).\n190\t\n191\t**Fallback to manual entry:**\n192\t- If the autocomplete API returns an error or times out (use a 2s timeout), show a non-blocking inline notice: *\"Address suggestions unavailable — please type your address manually.\"*\n193\t- All address fields remain fully editable regardless — autocomplete is an enhancement, not a gate\n194\t- Use `navigator.onLine` and a preflight `HEAD` request to detect offline state proactively\n195\t\n196\t**Autofill → field decomposition:**\n197\tWhen the user selects a suggestion, the Places API returns a structured object. Decompose it into individual form fields:\n198\t\n199\t```ts\n200\tconst handleSelect = async (placeId: string) => {\n201\t  const details = await getPlaceDetails(placeId) // street, city, state, zip, country\n202\t  form.setValue('address1', details.streetNumber + ' ' + details.route)\n203\t  form.setValue('city', details.locality)\n204\t  form.setValue('state', details.administrativeAreaLevel1)\n205\t  form.setValue('zip', details.postalCode)\n206\t  // Trigger validation on all filled fields\n207\t  form.trigger(['address1', 'city', 'state', 'zip'])\n208\t}\n209\t```\n210\t\n211\t**Server-side validation is mandatory:** Autofilled addresses must still go through USPS/SmartyStreets address verification on the server before order creation. The frontend presents a \"Did you mean: 123 Main St, Suite 4?\" correction flow if the server returns a normalized variant. Never trust the Places API as the authoritative address validator — it can autocomplete non-deliverable addresses.\n212\t\n213\t**Tradeoffs:** SmartyStreets is more accurate for USPS delivery validation but costs more. Google Places has better international coverage but requires a separate geocoding call to get full structured data. For global checkout, use Google Places for UX + a server-side validation step for each country's postal service API. The debounce delay is a UX-vs-cost tradeoff — 300ms feels natural; dropping to 150ms roughly doubles API costs.\n214\t\n215\t---\n216\t\n217\t### A user fills out the payment form and hits \"Pay Now,\" but the network request takes 3 seconds. Describe your loading state strategy: what is disabled, what is shown, and how do you prevent double-submission?\n218\t\n219\t**Problem framing:** A 3-second payment request is a trust-critical moment. If the user sees no feedback, they assume something broke and click again — causing a double-charge attempt. If you disable too much, they feel trapped. The loading state must be both reassuring and idempotent.\n220\t\n221\t**Approach:**\n222\t\n223\t**Immediately on click (0ms):**\n224\t1. Set `isSubmitting = true`\n225\t2. Disable the \"Pay Now\" button (prevent click replay)\n226\t3. Disable all form fields (prevent mutation mid-request)\n227\t4. Show a spinner *inside* the button (not replacing it — preserves button dimensions, prevents CLS)\n228\t5. Change button text to \"Processing…\"\n229\t\n230\t```tsx\n231\t<button\n232\t  type=\"submit\"\n233\t  disabled={isSubmitting}\n234\t  aria-busy={isSubmitting}\n235\t  aria-label={isSubmitting ? 'Processing payment…' : 'Pay Now'}\n236\t>\n237\t  {isSubmitting ? <Spinner size=\"sm\" /> : null}\n238\t  {isSubmitting ? 'Processing…' : 'Pay Now'}\n239\t</button>\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\t**At 1.5 seconds (if still pending):** Show a reassurance message below the form: *\"Your payment is being processed. Please don't close this window.\"* This threshold covers the \"did it work?\" anxiety window without spamming users on fast connections.\n243\t\n244\t**At 10 seconds (timeout):** Show an error with two options:\n245\t- *\"Check your order status\"* → link to `/orders?status=pending`\n246\t- *\"Try again\"* → re-enables the form\n247\t\n248\t**Double-submission prevention — three layers:**\n249\t1. **UI layer:** Button is `disabled` immediately on first click\n250\t2. **Request layer:** Generate an idempotency key on component mount (`crypto.randomUUID()`) and send it as a header on every payment attempt. The server deduplicates on this key — a second identical request returns the first response, not a second charge\n251\t3. **State machine guard:** `isSubmitting` is a state flag; the submit handler bails immediately if it's already true\n252\t\n253\t```ts\n254\tconst handleSubmit = async () => {\n255\t  if (isSubmitting) return  // guard\n256\t  setIsSubmitting(true)\n257\t  try {\n258\t    await confirmPayment({ idempotencyKey })\n259\t    navigate('/confirmation')\n260\t  } catch (err) {\n261\t    setError(err)\n262\t  } finally {\n263\t    setIsSubmitting(false)  // only on definitive failure\n264\t    // On success: navigate away, so no need to reset\n265\t  }\n266\t}\n267\t```\n268\t\n269\t**Tradeoffs:** `finally { setIsSubmitting(false) }` should only run on error — on success, the user is navigated away and resetting state is moot. Some teams skip the idempotency key for simplicity and rely solely on UI disabling — this is fine for most cases but fails if the user opens a second tab or the first click triggers two events (race on slow devices).\n270\t\n271\t---\n272\t\n273\t### How would you design the order summary panel in a checkout flow so it stays in sync with cart mutations (quantity changes, coupon application, tax recalculation) without full-page reloads?\n274\t\n275\t**Problem framing:** The order summary (subtotal, tax, shipping cost, total) is computed server-side based on factors the frontend can't fully know (tax jurisdiction rules, coupon eligibility, shipping rate matrix). Keeping it in sync without full reloads requires a clear contract between frontend cart state and server-computed totals.\n276\t\n277\t**Approach:**\n278\t\n279\t**Optimistic subtotal, authoritative total:**\n280\t- The frontend can compute `subtotal = Σ(price × qty)` instantly for snappy feedback\n281\t- `tax`, `shipping`, `discount`, and `total` come from the server and should never be fabricated client-side\n282\t\n283\t**Debounced recalculation:**\n284\t\n285\t```ts\n286\tconst recalculateOrder = useMemo(\n287\t  () => debounce(async (cart: CartState) => {\n288\t    setTotalsLoading(true)\n289\t    try {\n290\t      const totals = await api.post('/cart/calculate', {\n291\t        items: cart.items,\n292\t        coupon: cart.couponCode,\n293\t        shippingAddressId: cart.shippingAddress?.id,\n294\t      })\n295\t      setTotals(totals)\n296\t    } finally {\n297\t      setTotalsLoading(false)\n298\t    }\n299\t  }, 500),\n300\t  []\n301\t)\n302\t\n303\t// Fire on every cart mutation\n304\tuseEffect(() => { recalculateOrder(cart) }, [cart])\n305\t```\n306\t\n307\t500ms debounce prevents a recalc request on every quantity spinner tick. Show a subtle skeleton/shimmer on the tax and total rows while fetching — not on the subtotal (which the frontend already updated).\n308\t\n309\t**Coupon application:**\n310\t- Input + \"Apply\" button\n311\t- Optimistically show a \"Applying…\" state, then reconcile with server response\n312\t- If invalid: inline error *\"Coupon SAVE20 not valid for this cart\"*\n313\t- If valid: animate the discount line item appearing in the summary\n314\t\n315\t**Race condition guard:** Tag each recalculation request with a sequence number. On response, only apply if the sequence number matches the latest request (discard stale responses):\n316\t\n317\t```ts\n318\tconst seq = useRef(0)\n319\tconst recalc = async (cart) => {\n320\t  const thisSeq = ++seq.current\n321\t  const totals = await api.post('/cart/calculate', cart)\n322\t  if (thisSeq !== seq.current) return  // stale\n323\t  setTotals(totals)\n324\t}\n325\t```\n326\t\n327\t**Tradeoffs:** Debouncing 500ms means the total might be briefly stale after rapid quantity changes — acceptable since the subtotal updates instantly. An alternative is SSE-streaming totals in real time (too complex for marginal gain). Another alternative is computing tax client-side using a tax table — faster but error-prone and not compliant in jurisdictions with address-level tax rules.\n328\t\n329\t---\n330\t\n331\t### How do you approach progressive disclosure in a checkout form — e.g., showing the billing address form only when it differs from shipping, or revealing the installment options only for purchases above a threshold?\n332\t\n333\t**Problem framing:** Progressive disclosure reduces cognitive load and perceived form length, directly improving conversion. But it requires careful state management — conditionally rendered fields must not lose their values when hidden, and their validation rules must be active only when visible.\n334\t\n335\t**Approach:**\n336\t\n337\t**Billing address toggle:**\n338\t\n339\t```tsx\n340\t<label>\n341\t  <input\n342\t    type=\"checkbox\"\n343\t    checked={billingSameAsShipping}\n344\t    onChange={e => setBillingSameAsShipping(e.target.checked)}\n345\t  />\n346\t  Billing address same as shipping\n347\t</label>\n348\t\n349\t{!billingSameAsShipping && (\n350\t  <BillingAddressForm />\n351\t)}\n352\t```\n353\t\n354\tWhen `billingSameAsShipping` is true, the billing form is hidden *and* the billing address is set to the shipping address at submission time. The form fields are *unmounted* when hidden — this intentionally clears billing values since they're irrelevant. If you need to preserve the billing values (in case the user toggles back), keep the component mounted but visually hidden with `display: none`, and use `aria-hidden`:\n355\t\n356\t```tsx\n357\t<div hidden={billingSameAsShipping} aria-hidden={billingSameAsShipping}>\n358\t  <BillingAddressForm />\n359\t</div>\n360\t```\n361\t\n362\t**Validation scope:** When the billing form is hidden, its validation rules must not fire on submit. Use a `shouldValidate` flag in your schema:\n363\t\n364\t```ts\n365\t// With Zod:\n366\tconst schema = z.object({\n367\t  billing: billingSameAsShipping\n368\t    ? z.object({}).optional()\n369\t    : billingAddressSchema,\n370\t})\n371\t```\n372\t\n373\tOr with React Hook Form's `shouldUnregister: true` — fields unregister themselves when unmounted, removing them from validation automatically.\n374\t\n375\t**Installment options (above threshold):**\n376\t\n377\t```tsx\n378\t{cart.total >= 5000_00 && ( // $5,000 in cents\n379\t  <InstallmentSelector options={installmentPlans} />\n380\t)}\n381\t```\n382\t\n383\tThe threshold comes from a feature-flag config (not hardcoded), allowing the risk team to adjust it without a deploy. Animate the reveal with `framer-motion` or a CSS transition to avoid a jarring layout shift.\n384\t\n385\t**Tradeoffs:** `shouldUnregister: true` (unmount = unregister) is simpler but loses user-entered data on toggle. `shouldUnregister: false` (mount once, hide) preserves data but means hidden fields can submit stale values if not explicitly nulled. Choose based on whether \"remembering\" the value when the user re-expands is a feature or a footgun (for billing addresses, remembering is usually good UX).\n386\t\n387\t---\n388\t\n389\t## Deep\n390\t\n391\t### Stripe's \"Link\" feature auto-fills payment details for returning customers across merchants. If you were building a similar saved-payment-method flow, how would you design the UX handoff between \"guest checkout\" and \"recognized user\" without causing a jarring re-render mid-form, and how do you handle the case where the saved method has expired?\n392\t\n393\t**Problem framing:** Stripe Link identifies users by email across merchants and auto-fills their saved card details. The UX challenge is that recognition happens asynchronously *after* the user starts typing their email, potentially in the middle of a form they've already partially filled. A clumsy handoff — clearing fields, full re-render, or a modal interruption — damages trust at the highest-value moment in checkout.\n394\t\n395\t**Approach:**\n396\t\n397\t**Detection flow:**\n398\t\n399\t```mermaid\n400\tsequenceDiagram\n401\t  participant User\n402\t  participant Form\n403\t  participant API\n404\t  participant AuthService\n405\t\n406\t  User->>Form: Types email (blur)\n407\t  Form->>API: POST /user/lookup { email }\n408\t  API->>AuthService: Check if recognized user\n409\t  AuthService-->>API: { recognized: true, hasValidMethod: true }\n410\t  API-->>Form: { recognized, methods: [...] }\n411\t  Form->>User: Show \"Welcome back, Sarah\" inline (no re-render)\n412\t```\n413\t\n414\t**Non-jarring handoff — key techniques:**\n415\t\n416\t1. **Don't re-render the form** — When the user is recognized, don't unmount the guest form and mount a new one. Instead, animate a \"recognized user\" panel *above* or *below* the email field using CSS transitions. The existing form fields stay mounted and focused.\n417\t\n418\t2. **Offer, don't force** — Show: *\"We found a saved card ending in 4242. Use it or continue as guest.\"* Two buttons. This respects the user's agency and handles the case where the browser auto-filled a different email.\n419\t\n420\t3. **Authentication gate:** Accessing saved payment methods requires a soft authentication (magic link OTP via SMS/email or biometric on mobile WebAuthn). This challenge should appear in-flow as an inline step, not a redirect:\n421\t\n422\t```tsx\n423\t{recognizedUser && !authenticated && (\n424\t  <InlineOTPChallenge\n425\t    email={recognizedUser.email}\n426\t    onVerified={() => loadSavedMethods()}\n427\t  />\n428\t)}\n429\t```\n430\t\n431\t**Handling expired saved methods:**\n432\t\n433\t- Check expiry client-side before presenting the method: `new Date(method.expiryYear, method.expiryMonth - 1) < new Date()`\n434\t- If expired: surface a specific message — *\"Your saved card ending in 4242 expired in March. Please use a new card.\"* — rather than letting the user click \"Pay\" and receive a decline\n435\t- If multiple saved methods, filter out expired ones and show only valid ones\n436\t- Offer \"Update card\" which opens an inline card update form (new Stripe Elements instance) rather than redirecting to a profile page\n437\t\n438\t**Pre-populating non-sensitive fields:** Billing name, address, and email from the saved profile can safely populate form fields — no security concern. Only the card number/CVV remain inside Stripe's iframe and are never exposed to your form.\n439\t\n440\t**Tradeoffs:** The \"soft auth via OTP\" flow adds friction — some users won't complete it and will fall back to guest checkout. This is acceptable; the fallback must be seamless. An alternative is cookie-based recognition (no OTP required) — lower friction but more fraud risk since cookies can be stolen. Stripe Link uses a combination of cookie + device fingerprint to calibrate the required auth level.\n441\t\n442\t---\n443\t\n444\t### A/B tests on checkout forms consistently show that reducing the number of visible fields increases conversion — but fewer fields can also increase fraud and failed deliveries. How do you architect a checkout form component system that allows product and risk teams to toggle field visibility, validation rules, and required/optional status from a feature-flag config without frontend deploys?\n445\t\n446\t**Problem framing:** The tension between conversion optimization (fewer fields) and fraud/fulfillment risk (more data) can't be resolved statically. Different user segments, merchants, and risk profiles need different field configurations. Hard-coding field layout in components means every A/B test requires a deploy.\n447\t\n448\t**Approach:**\n449\t\n450\t**Config-driven field schema:**\n451\t\n452\t```ts\n453\tinterface FieldConfig {\n454\t  id: string\n455\t  type: 'text' | 'select' | 'checkbox' | 'phone' | 'postal'\n456\t  label: string\n457\t  visible: boolean\n458\t  required: boolean\n459\t  validators: ValidatorId[]           // e.g. ['minLength:2', 'postalFormat']\n460\t  visibilityCondition?: ConditionExpr // e.g. { field: 'country', eq: 'US' }\n461\t}\n462\t\n463\tinterface CheckoutFormConfig {\n464\t  version: string\n465\t  fields: FieldConfig[]\n466\t  experimentId?: string\n467\t}\n468\t```\n469\t\n470\tThis config is fetched from a feature-flag service (LaunchDarkly, Statsig) on checkout page load. The frontend renders whatever the config says — it has no hardcoded opinions about which fields exist.\n471\t\n472\t**Config renderer:**\n473\t\n474\t```tsx\n475\tfunction DynamicCheckoutForm({ config }: { config: CheckoutFormConfig }) {\n476\t  return (\n477\t    <form>\n478\t      {config.fields\n479\t        .filter(f => evaluateCondition(f.visibilityCondition, formValues))\n480\t        .filter(f => f.visible)\n481\t        .map(field => (\n482\t          <DynamicField\n483\t            key={field.id}\n484\t            config={field}\n485\t            required={field.required}\n486\t            validators={resolveValidators(field.validators)}\n487\t          />\n488\t        ))\n489\t      }\n490\t    </form>\n491\t  )\n492\t}\n493\t```\n494\t\n495\t**Validator registry:** Validators are identified by string ID and resolved from a registry at runtime, not baked into component code:\n496\t\n497\t```ts\n498\tconst VALIDATORS: Record<string, Validator> = {\n499\t  'minLength:2': v => v.length >= 2 || 'Too short',\n500\t  'postalFormat': v => /^\\d{5}(-\\d{4})?$/.test(v) || 'Invalid ZIP',\n501\t  'phoneE164': v => /^\\+[1-9]\\d{10,14}$/.test(v) || 'Invalid phone',\n502\t}\n503\t```\n504\t\n505\t**Safety rails:** The risk team can add fields via config, but cannot add fields that collect card data (those are always handled by Stripe Elements, not the dynamic form). The form config system is scoped to non-PCI data. A server-side config validation step rejects any config that attempts to add a `cardNumber` field type.\n506\t\n507\t**Analytics integration:** Each rendered config version includes the `experimentId`. All analytics events include this ID so the data team can attribute conversion rates to specific form configurations.\n508\t\n509\t**Tradeoffs:** Config-driven forms shift validation bugs from \"caught at PR review\" to \"caught at runtime.\" Mitigate with a config schema (Zod) validated on the feature flag SDK's response. The main risk is configuration drift — a risk team member toggling a field to `required: false` without understanding downstream fraud impact. Solve with a config review workflow (PR-gated config changes) even if the feature flag itself deploys without a code deploy.\n510\t\n511\t---\n512\t\n513\t### Describe how you would implement a \"smart retry\" UX after a card decline. Specifically: how do you preserve the user's previously entered data (without re-storing raw card numbers), which fields do you clear vs. keep, and what messaging hierarchy do you use to distinguish a soft decline (insufficient funds) from a hard decline (stolen card) given that your frontend may only receive a generic error code?\n514\t\n515\t**Problem framing:** A card decline is not the end of the checkout — 30–40% of declines result in a successful retry with the same or a different card. The UX goal is to keep the user engaged, give them enough information to act, and preserve their non-sensitive data to reduce re-entry friction. The constraint is that raw card data never touches your state — it lives in Stripe's iframe.\n516\t\n517\t**Approach:**\n518\t\n519\t**What to clear vs. keep on decline:**\n520\t\n521\t| Field | Action | Reason |\n522\t|-------|--------|--------|\n523\t| Card number | Stripe Elements auto-clears | Stripe's iframe handles this; you cannot clear it |\n524\t| Expiry | Stripe Elements keeps | Often still valid; user can change if needed |\n525\t| CVV | Stripe Elements auto-clears | Security requirement — never persisted |\n526\t| Name on card | Keep | Non-sensitive; tedious to re-type |\n527\t| Billing address | Keep | Non-sensitive; very tedious to re-type |\n528\t| Email | Keep | Non-sensitive |\n529\t| Coupon code | Keep | User shouldn't re-enter a coupon they already applied |\n530\t\n531\t**PaymentIntent preservation:** On retry, do NOT create a new PaymentIntent. Update the existing one with the new payment method:\n532\t\n533\t```ts\n534\t// On retry: attach new card to same PaymentIntent\n535\tawait stripe.confirmCardPayment(existingClientSecret, {\n536\t  payment_method: { card: cardElement }\n537\t})\n538\t// Same PaymentIntent = same idempotency guarantee\n539\t```\n540\t\n541\t**Messaging hierarchy:**\n542\t\n543\tThe frontend typically receives only the top-level `card_declined` code. Stripe provides a `decline_code` with more detail, but not all codes are safe to show users verbatim:\n544\t\n545\t```ts\n546\tconst DECLINE_MESSAGES: Record<string, { user: string; showRetry: boolean }> = {\n547\t  insufficient_funds: {\n548\t    user: 'Your card has insufficient funds. Try a different card or contact your bank.',\n549\t    showRetry: true,\n550\t  },\n551\t  card_velocity_exceeded: {\n552\t    user: 'Too many attempts on this card. Please try a different card.',\n553\t    showRetry: true,\n554\t  },\n555\t  stolen_card: {\n556\t    // DO NOT tell the user the card is flagged as stolen — merchant guidance from card networks\n557\t    user: 'We were unable to process this card. Please try a different payment method.',\n558\t    showRetry: true,\n559\t  },\n560\t  do_not_honor: {\n561\t    user: 'Your bank has declined this payment. Please contact your bank or try a different card.',\n562\t    showRetry: true,\n563\t  },\n564\t  fraudulent: {\n565\t    // High-risk: consider not offering retry at all\n566\t    user: 'We were unable to complete your purchase. Please contact support.',\n567\t    showRetry: false,\n568\t  },\n569\t}\n570\t\n571\t// Fallback\n572\tconst DEFAULT_MESSAGE = {\n573\t  user: 'Your payment was declined. Please try a different card or contact your bank.',\n574\t  showRetry: true,\n575\t}\n576\t```\n577\t\n578\t**Retry cap:** After 3 failed attempts, lock the checkout and require the user to start over (new session). This prevents card testing attacks. The cap is enforced both client-side (UI logic) and server-side (PaymentIntent metadata tracks attempt count).\n579\t\n580\t**Retry UX flow:**\n581\t\n582\t```mermaid\n583\tflowchart TD\n584\t    Decline[Card Declined] --> ShowMessage[Show decline message with reason]\n585\t    ShowMessage --> SoftDecline{Soft decline?}\n586\t    SoftDecline -->|Yes| KeepForm[Keep form, clear card field only]\n587\t    SoftDecline -->|No| SuggestNew[Suggest trying different card]\n588\t    KeepForm --> RetryBtn[Enable Retry button]\n589\t    SuggestNew --> RetryBtn\n590\t    RetryBtn --> AttemptCount{Attempts < 3?}\n591\t    AttemptCount -->|Yes| ResubmitSamePI[confirmCardPayment on same PI]\n592\t    AttemptCount -->|No| LockForm[Lock form, show support link]\n593\t```\n594\t\n595\t**Tradeoffs:** Showing specific decline codes (e.g. `insufficient_funds`) is more helpful but can embarrass users. Card network rules actually prohibit revealing `stolen_card` status to the user — always show a generic message for security-flagged codes. The retry cap (3 attempts) is a judgment call — too low and legitimate users get frustrated; too high and you're enabling card testing. Stripe's default recommendation is 3–5 attempts.\n596\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01XjZ9HT3i2UMuwALeh5JgXq","session_id":"32aa5972-b92a-4580-b5c5-f0297ce83ccb","uuid":"c409df71-07b4-4517-82e2-c8a1fbd90343","timestamp":"2026-06-20T19:05:09.522Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write answers for payments-system section 05: Performance & Core Web Vitals"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_019X8R5G3AopgVD6F5ssJq97","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 04 — Real-Time Updates & Webhooks\n2\t\n3\tPayment status is never instantaneous. Stripe's `confirmCardPayment` returns within milliseconds, but the authoritative settlement signal — the webhook — arrives asynchronously, sometimes seconds later, sometimes minutes, and for certain payment methods like SEPA debit or ACH, days. This gap between \"the user clicked Pay\" and \"the bank confirmed the charge\" is where most real-time complexity lives. This section covers the full architecture of delivering payment status updates reliably across every frontend context: active tabs, closed-and-reopened tabs, mobile apps in the background, and concurrent POS dashboards processing dozens of payments simultaneously. The recurring theme is that the webhook is ground truth — everything on the client is provisional until the server confirms it.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Core Questions\n8\t\n9\t### Q1. Reconciling `confirmCardPayment` return vs. webhook ground truth\n10\t\n11\t**Problem framing:** `stripe.confirmCardPayment()` is a synchronous-feeling call that resolves with a `PaymentIntent` object whose `status` field says `succeeded`. This is not the same as the Stripe webhook event `payment_intent.succeeded`. The JS SDK call returns after Stripe's API has created and confirmed the charge object, but \"confirmed\" here means Stripe accepted the request and it is in flight — not that the issuing bank has settled it. The webhook arrives after the issuing bank communicates the final outcome back through the card network. For most cards this gap is milliseconds to a few seconds. For certain card programs (deferred debit, some international networks) the gap is longer, and in all cases the webhook is what your backend actually acts on to fulfill the order. If a user sees a success screen based only on the client-side return value but the webhook never arrives (network failure, Stripe retry exhausted, your endpoint returned 500), you may fulfill an order with no settled funds.\n12\t\n13\t**Approach:**\n14\t\n15\tThe reconciliation architecture works in two layers: client-side optimism gated by server confirmation.\n16\t\n17\t```mermaid\n18\tsequenceDiagram\n19\t    participant Browser\n20\t    participant YourAPI as Your Backend\n21\t    participant Stripe\n22\t\n23\t    Browser->>Stripe: stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret)\n24\t    Stripe-->>Browser: PaymentIntent { status: \"succeeded\" }\n25\t    Note over Browser: DO NOT show success yet\n26\t    Browser->>YourAPI: POST /api/payment/confirm { paymentIntentId }\n27\t    Stripe->>YourAPI: POST /webhooks/stripe (payment_intent.succeeded)\n28\t    YourAPI->>YourAPI: Verify signature, write to DB, fulfill order\n29\t    YourAPI-->>Browser: { status: \"succeeded\", orderId: \"ord_xxx\" }\n30\t    Note over Browser: NOW show success screen\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tAfter `confirmCardPayment` resolves with `status: \"succeeded\"`, immediately call your own backend to confirm:\n34\t\n35\t```javascript\n36\tasync function handlePaymentConfirmation(clientSecret, stripe) {\n37\t  const { paymentIntent, error } = await stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {\n38\t    payment_method: { card: cardElement },\n39\t  });\n40\t\n41\t  if (error) {\n42\t    return { outcome: 'failed', error };\n43\t  }\n44\t\n45\t  // Client-side says succeeded — now verify server-side\n46\t  // Show \"Confirming your payment...\" — not success yet\n47\t  setStatus('verifying');\n48\t\n49\t  const serverConfirmation = await pollServerStatus(paymentIntent.id, {\n50\t    maxAttempts: 12,\n51\t    initialDelayMs: 500,\n52\t  });\n53\t\n54\t  if (serverConfirmation.status === 'succeeded') {\n55\t    // Webhook was received and processed — safe to show success\n56\t    return { outcome: 'succeeded', orderId: serverConfirmation.orderId };\n57\t  }\n58\t\n59\t  return { outcome: 'processing', paymentIntentId: paymentIntent.id };\n60\t}\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\tYour backend `/api/payment/status/:piId` endpoint reads from your own database (which the webhook populates), not from Stripe directly on every poll. This is crucial: polling Stripe's API directly on every client request is rate-limited, slow, and bypasses your own business logic (fulfillment, inventory, etc.).\n64\t\n65\t**The safe rule for showing a success screen:** You may show success when your backend confirms the webhook has been processed **and** your fulfillment logic has run. Until then, show a \"verifying\" / \"confirming\" intermediate state. The user-visible delay is typically under 2 seconds.\n66\t\n67\t**Tradeoffs:** A small percentage of payments will have the client-side `succeeded` status but the webhook delayed (Stripe retries webhooks over 72 hours if your endpoint returns errors). For these users, polling will eventually time out. The correct UX is to show a \"We're confirming your payment — you'll receive a confirmation email shortly\" message, not an error. The webhook always eventually arrives or Stripe notifies you of permanent failure. Do not make the user wait indefinitely — set them free after ~60 seconds of polling with the email-confirmation fallback.\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t### Q2. Polling vs. WebSockets vs. SSE for payment status\n72\t\n73\t**Problem framing:** Once a payment enters an async state (sent for processing, waiting for webhook), the frontend needs a mechanism to receive the resolution without requiring the user to manually refresh. The three primary options — polling, WebSockets, and Server-Sent Events — have meaningfully different complexity, infrastructure, and reliability profiles. The choice matters especially in payment flows because the connection must be resilient (mobile networks are unreliable), the message stream is unidirectional (server → client), and the use case is ephemeral (one confirmation per session, not a persistent data stream).\n74\t\n75\t**Approach:**\n76\t\n77\t| Dimension | Polling | SSE | WebSocket |\n78\t|-----------|---------|-----|-----------|\n79\t| Direction | Client-initiated | Server-initiated (one-way) | Bidirectional |\n80\t| Protocol | HTTP | HTTP/2 (or HTTP/1.1) | WS (TCP upgrade) |\n81\t| Built-in reconnect | ❌ Manual | ✅ `EventSource` native | ❌ Manual |\n82\t| Proxy/firewall friendly | ✅ | ✅ (pure HTTP) | ⚠️ Some proxies strip WS |\n83\t| Infrastructure complexity | Low | Low | High (stateful server) |\n84\t| Mobile battery impact | High (keep-alive requests) | Low (idle connection) | Moderate |\n85\t| Implementation effort | Minimal | Low | High |\n86\t| Right for payment status | Fallback | ✅ Primary choice | Overkill for one-way |\n87\t\n88\t**SSE is the right choice for payment confirmation flows.** The reasons:\n89\t\n90\t1. **Unidirectional fit:** Payment status updates flow server → client. There is no reason the client needs to send data over the same channel. WebSockets' bidirectional capability is wasted here and adds overhead.\n91\t\n92\t2. **Native reconnection:** `EventSource` reconnects automatically on connection drop, which is critical on mobile. The browser re-issues the request with a `Last-Event-ID` header so you can replay missed events.\n93\t\n94\t3. **HTTP semantics:** SSE runs over plain HTTP/2. Load balancers, CDNs, and proxies that handle HTTP handle SSE without special configuration. WebSockets require explicit proxy support and upgraded connections.\n95\t\n96\t4. **Simple infrastructure:** SSE is a long-lived HTTP response — your existing HTTP server handles it. WebSockets require either a dedicated WS server or a library like Socket.IO with room management.\n97\t\n98\tSSE implementation for payment status:\n99\t\n100\t```javascript\n101\t// Client\n102\tfunction subscribeToPaymentStatus(paymentIntentId, onUpdate) {\n103\t  const url = `/api/payment-status/stream?pi=${paymentIntentId}`;\n104\t  const source = new EventSource(url);\n105\t\n106\t  source.addEventListener('payment.succeeded', (event) => {\n107\t    const data = JSON.parse(event.data);\n108\t    onUpdate({ status: 'succeeded', orderId: data.orderId });\n109\t    source.close(); // One-shot — close after resolution\n110\t  });\n111\t\n112\t  source.addEventListener('payment.failed', (event) => {\n113\t    const data = JSON.parse(event.data);\n114\t    onUpdate({ status: 'failed', declineCode: data.decline_code });\n115\t    source.close();\n116\t  });\n117\t\n118\t  source.onerror = () => {\n119\t    // EventSource reconnects automatically — onerror fires on temporary drops\n120\t    // Only close if you want to give up entirely\n121\t  };\n122\t\n123\t  // Return cleanup function for useEffect\n124\t  return () => source.close();\n125\t}\n126\t```\n127\t\n128\t```javascript\n129\t// Server (Node/Express)\n130\tapp.get('/api/payment-status/stream', (req, res) => {\n131\t  const { pi: paymentIntentId } = req.query;\n132\t\n133\t  res.writeHead(200, {\n134\t    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',\n135\t    'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',\n136\t    'Connection': 'keep-alive',\n137\t    'X-Accel-Buffering': 'no', // Disable nginx buffering\n138\t  });\n139\t\n140\t  // Register this connection in your pub/sub store\n141\t  const cleanup = pubSub.subscribe(`payment:${paymentIntentId}`, (event) => {\n142\t    res.write(`event: ${event.type}\\n`);\n143\t    res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(event.data)}\\n\\n`);\n144\t    res.end();\n145\t  });\n146\t\n147\t  req.on('close', cleanup);\n148\t});\n149\t```\n150\t\n151\t**When to use polling instead:** As a fallback when SSE is unavailable (HTTP/1.0 intermediaries, some corporate proxies that buffer chunked responses). Implement SSE as primary; detect connection failure and fall back to polling automatically.\n152\t\n153\t**When to use WebSockets:** Multi-directional flows — a POS dashboard where the client also sends commands (void payment, split bill), or collaborative cart editing. Not appropriate for a simple payment confirmation screen.\n154\t\n155\t**Tradeoffs:** SSE has a browser limit of 6 concurrent connections per origin (HTTP/1.1) — not an issue for a payment confirmation page with one connection, but a concern for dashboards with many concurrent streams. HTTP/2 multiplexing removes this limit. SSE also doesn't work in Internet Explorer — acceptable for most modern payment contexts.\n156\t\n157\t---\n158\t\n159\t### Q3. Resilient polling strategy with exponential backoff\n160\t\n161\t**Problem framing:** Simple interval-based polling (`setInterval(fetch, 3000)`) has three failure modes: it hammers the server uniformly under load (no backoff), it doesn't handle the server being temporarily unavailable, and it runs indefinitely if the user leaves the page. A production-grade polling loop needs exponential backoff with jitter, a maximum attempt cap with graceful UX degradation, and cleanup on component unmount via `AbortController`.\n162\t\n163\t**Approach:**\n164\t\n165\tComplete polling implementation:\n166\t\n167\t```javascript\n168\tasync function pollPaymentStatus(paymentIntentId, { onResolved, onTimeout, onError }) {\n169\t  const MAX_ATTEMPTS = 12;        // ~2 minutes total with exponential backoff\n170\t  const BASE_DELAY_MS = 1000;     // Start at 1s\n171\t  const CAP_DELAY_MS = 30_000;    // Cap at 30s\n172\t  const controller = new AbortController();\n173\t\n174\t  // Expose abort for cleanup\n175\t  pollPaymentStatus.abort = () => controller.abort();\n176\t\n177\t  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {\n178\t    // Delay before each poll (0ms on first attempt)\n179\t    if (attempt > 0) {\n180\t      const exponentialDelay = BASE_DELAY_MS * Math.pow(2, attempt - 1);\n181\t      const cappedDelay = Math.min(exponentialDelay, CAP_DELAY_MS);\n182\t      // Add jitter: ±20% randomness to prevent thundering herd\n183\t      const jitter = cappedDelay * 0.2 * (Math.random() - 0.5);\n184\t      await sleep(cappedDelay + jitter, controller.signal);\n185\t    }\n186\t\n187\t    if (controller.signal.aborted) return;\n188\t\n189\t    try {\n190\t      const response = await fetch(`/api/payment/status/${paymentIntentId}`, {\n191\t        signal: controller.signal,\n192\t      });\n193\t\n194\t      if (!response.ok) {\n195\t        // 5xx: server error — retry\n196\t        if (response.status >= 500) continue;\n197\t        // 4xx: client error (404 = PI not found) — stop\n198\t        onError({ type: 'client_error', status: response.status });\n199\t        return;\n200\t      }\n201\t\n202\t      const { status, orderId, declineCode } = await response.json();\n203\t\n204\t      if (status === 'succeeded') {\n205\t        onResolved({ status, orderId });\n206\t        return;\n207\t      }\n208\t\n209\t      if (status === 'failed' || status === 'canceled') {\n210\t        onResolved({ status, declineCode });\n211\t        return;\n212\t      }\n213\t\n214\t      // status === 'processing' — continue polling\n215\t      // Update UI with attempt progress for transparency\n216\t      onError?.({ type: 'still_processing', attempt, maxAttempts: MAX_ATTEMPTS });\n217\t\n218\t    } catch (err) {\n219\t      if (err.name === 'AbortError') return; // Intentional cancellation\n220\t\n221\t      // Network error — retry (don't stop)\n222\t      console.warn(`Poll attempt ${attempt + 1} failed:`, err.message);\n223\t    }\n224\t  }\n225\t\n226\t  // Exhausted all attempts\n227\t  onTimeout({ attempts: MAX_ATTEMPTS });\n228\t}\n229\t\n230\tfunction sleep(ms, signal) {\n231\t  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n232\t    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);\n233\t    signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {\n234\t      clearTimeout(timer);\n235\t      reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));\n236\t    });\n237\t  });\n238\t}\n239\t```\n240\t\n241\t**Backoff schedule at each attempt:**\n242\t\n243\t| Attempt | Base delay | With cap | With ±20% jitter range |\n244\t|---------|-----------|----------|----------------------|\n245\t| 1 | 0s | 0s | 0s |\n246\t| 2 | 1s | 1s | 0.8s – 1.2s |\n247\t| 3 | 2s | 2s | 1.6s – 2.4s |\n248\t| 4 | 4s | 4s | 3.2s – 4.8s |\n249\t| 5 | 8s | 8s | 6.4s – 9.6s |\n250\t| 6 | 16s | 16s | 12.8s – 19.2s |\n251\t| 7+ | 32s → | 30s (cap) | 24s – 36s |\n252\t\n253\t12 attempts ≈ 0 + 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 30 + 30 + 30 + 30 + 30 + 30 = ~211 seconds ≈ 3.5 minutes.\n254\t\n255\t**UX at each stage:**\n256\t\n257\t```\n258\tAttempts 1–3 (0–3s):    \"Confirming your payment...\" + spinner\n259\tAttempts 4–7 (3–30s):   \"Still confirming... this is taking a moment.\"\n260\tAttempts 8–11 (30–90s): \"Almost there — your bank is still responding.\"\n261\t                         + \"Having trouble? Check your email shortly.\"\n262\tAttempt 12 / timeout:   \"We're still waiting on confirmation.\"\n263\t                         + \"You'll receive an email when your payment is complete.\"\n264\t                         + [Check Order Status] button\n265\t```\n266\t\n267\t**React integration with cleanup:**\n268\t\n269\t```javascript\n270\tuseEffect(() => {\n271\t  pollPaymentStatus(paymentIntentId, {\n272\t    onResolved: (result) => setPaymentResult(result),\n273\t    onTimeout: () => setStatus('email_pending'),\n274\t    onError: ({ attempt }) => setProgress(attempt),\n275\t  });\n276\t\n277\t  return () => pollPaymentStatus.abort(); // Cleanup on unmount\n278\t}, [paymentIntentId]);\n279\t```\n280\t\n281\t**Tradeoffs:** The jitter is critical — without it, every user whose payment entered `processing` at the same time (a common pattern after a marketing email blast) polls at synchronized intervals, creating load spikes. The `AbortController` cleanup is not optional: without it, the polling loop fires into unmounted components, causing React's \"setState on unmounted component\" warnings and wasted server requests. The 12-attempt / ~3.5-minute cap is generous; most resolutions happen within 30 seconds.\n282\t\n283\t---\n284\t\n285\t### Q4. The risk of trusting `confirmCardPayment` return value alone\n286\t\n287\t**Problem framing:** A backend that fulfills orders based solely on what the frontend claims — rather than verifying via webhook or a server-side Stripe API call — is vulnerable to a category of fraud where the frontend's report of success is fabricated or manipulated. This is not theoretical: client-side JavaScript is fully inspectable and modifiable.\n288\t\n289\t**Approach — the specific attack scenario:**\n290\t\n291\t**Client-side fake success attack:**\n292\t\n293\t```\n294\t1. Attacker opens your checkout page\n295\t2. They open DevTools → Network tab\n296\t3. They add a Payment Method using a card they control (valid but with $0 balance)\n297\t4. stripe.confirmCardPayment() returns { error: { code: 'card_declined' } }\n298\t5. Attacker sets a breakpoint in your payment confirmation handler BEFORE the\n299\t   success check, or uses a Proxy (mitmproxy/Charles) to intercept the response\n300\t6. They modify the response to appear as:\n301\t   { paymentIntent: { status: 'succeeded', id: 'pi_real_id_here' } }\n302\t7. Your frontend reads paymentIntent.status === 'succeeded' and:\n303\t   - Calls POST /api/order/fulfill with the (real) paymentIntentId\n304\t   - Your backend trusts the client's claim, marks order as paid\n305\t   - Fulfills the order (ships physical goods or grants digital access)\n306\t8. Your Stripe dashboard shows the PaymentIntent as declined. No charge occurred.\n307\t   Revenue loss = full order value.\n308\t```\n309\t\n310\tThis attack works because:\n311\t- The PaymentIntent ID is real (it exists on Stripe, but in `requires_payment_method` status)\n312\t- Your backend receives a real PI ID but never checks its actual status with Stripe\n313\t- The fraud signal only appears in Stripe's dashboard, not in your order system\n314\t\n315\t**The correct server-side verification pattern:**\n316\t\n317\t```javascript\n318\t// ❌ VULNERABLE: Trusting client report\n319\tapp.post('/api/order/fulfill', async (req, res) => {\n320\t  const { paymentIntentId } = req.body;\n321\t  // Just trust whatever the client says\n322\t  await fulfillOrder(req.body.orderId);\n323\t  res.json({ success: true });\n324\t});\n325\t\n326\t// ✅ CORRECT: Verify with Stripe directly or via webhook\n327\tapp.post('/api/order/fulfill', async (req, res) => {\n328\t  const { paymentIntentId, orderId } = req.body;\n329\t\n330\t  // Option A: Verify directly (for cases where webhook hasn't arrived yet)\n331\t  const pi = await stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(paymentIntentId);\n332\t  if (pi.status !== 'succeeded') {\n333\t    return res.status(402).json({ error: 'Payment not confirmed', status: pi.status });\n334\t  }\n335\t\n336\t  // Option B (preferred): Check your own DB — only populated by verified webhook\n337\t  const payment = await db.payments.findOne({ stripePaymentIntentId: paymentIntentId });\n338\t  if (!payment || payment.status !== 'succeeded') {\n339\t    return res.status(402).json({ error: 'Payment not confirmed' });\n340\t  }\n341\t\n342\t  await fulfillOrder(orderId);\n343\t  res.json({ success: true });\n344\t});\n345\t```\n346\t\n347\t**The webhook-as-fulfillment-trigger pattern is more secure than direct API verify:**\n348\t\n349\t```javascript\n350\t// Webhook handler — the only path to order fulfillment\n351\tapp.post('/webhooks/stripe', async (req, res) => {\n352\t  const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\n353\t  let event;\n354\t\n355\t  try {\n356\t    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET);\n357\t  } catch (err) {\n358\t    return res.status(400).send(`Webhook signature verification failed: ${err.message}`);\n359\t  }\n360\t\n361\t  if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {\n362\t    const pi = event.data.object;\n363\t    // This path is cryptographically signed — cannot be faked\n364\t    await db.payments.updateStatus(pi.id, 'succeeded');\n365\t    await fulfillOrder(pi.metadata.orderId); // orderId stored in PI metadata at creation\n366\t  }\n367\t\n368\t  res.json({ received: true });\n369\t});\n370\t```\n371\t\n372\t**Second attack scenario — race condition exploit:**\n373\t\n374\tEven with server-side checking, a race condition attack is possible if you check Stripe immediately and the attacker's PI is in `processing`:\n375\t\n376\t1. Attacker initiates a real charge that immediately enters `processing`\n377\t2. Frontend immediately calls `/api/order/fulfill`\n378\t3. Your server calls `stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve()` — sees `processing`\n379\t4. Your server (incorrectly) fulfills the order on `processing` rather than waiting for `succeeded`\n380\t5. The charge eventually fails; you've already shipped\n381\t\n382\t**Mitigation:** Never fulfill on `processing`. Only fulfill on `payment_intent.succeeded` webhook. Treat `processing` as \"pending\" from your fulfillment system's perspective.\n383\t\n384\t**Tradeoffs:** Verifying directly with Stripe on every `/fulfill` call adds ~200ms latency and counts against your Stripe API rate limits. The webhook-driven fulfillment pattern is more scalable: webhooks are async and don't add latency to the user's checkout response. The tradeoff is the brief window between the user seeing success (client-side) and the webhook arriving to actually fulfill — which is why the \"verifying\" intermediate state is important: it gives the webhook time to arrive before showing the user a success screen.\n385\t\n386\t---\n387\t\n388\t### Q5. \"Processing\" state UX for async payment methods\n389\t\n390\t**Problem framing:** Bank transfers, SEPA Direct Debit, ACH, Bacs, and certain card networks (some deferred debit programs) return a `processing` status from `confirmCardPayment` that can persist for hours to days before resolving. Keeping a user on a spinner for 3 business days is not an option. The UX must gracefully release the user while maintaining the ability to resurface the outcome when it eventually arrives.\n391\t\n392\t**Approach:**\n393\t\n394\tThe `processing` state requires a different UX contract than card payments: instead of \"wait for confirmation,\" the contract is \"we've received your payment, we'll tell you when it settles.\"\n395\t\n396\t**Immediate screen (0–30 seconds):**\n397\t\n398\tShow a \"Payment received\" screen (not \"Payment confirmed\" — semantically different):\n399\t\n400\t```\n401\t╔══════════════════════════════════════╗\n402\t║  ⏳ Payment Received                  ║\n403\t║                                      ║\n404\t║  Your payment is being processed.    ║\n405\t║  Bank transfers typically take       ║\n406\t║  1–3 business days to confirm.       ║\n407\t║                                      ║\n408\t║  Order #12345 has been created.      ║\n409\t║  We'll email you at user@email.com   ║\n410\t║  when payment is confirmed.          ║\n411\t║                                      ║\n412\t║  [View Order Details]  [Continue Shopping]  ║\n413\t╚══════════════════════════════════════╝\n414\t```\n415\t\n416\tKey UX decisions:\n417\t1. **Create the order immediately** in `payment_pending` state — don't wait for settlement. The user gets an order number they can reference with support.\n418\t2. **Set clear expectations** about timeline based on payment method type.\n419\t3. **Give them an exit** — \"Continue Shopping\" — so they don't feel trapped.\n420\t4. **Commit to an email notification.** This is the primary async notification channel.\n421\t\n422\t**Timer-based state progression:**\n423\t\n424\t```javascript\n425\tfunction ProcessingPaymentScreen({ paymentIntentId, orderId, paymentMethod }) {\n426\t  const [phase, setPhase] = useState('initial'); // initial | polling | release\n427\t\n428\t  const timeouts = {\n429\t    'card': 60,        // 60s before releasing user\n430\t    'sepa_debit': 0,   // Release immediately — takes days\n431\t    'ach': 0,          // Release immediately — takes 1-3 days\n432\t    'bank_transfer': 0,\n433\t  };\n434\t\n435\t  const holdDurationMs = (timeouts[paymentMethod] ?? 30) * 1000;\n436\t\n437\t  useEffect(() => {\n438\t    if (holdDurationMs === 0) {\n439\t      setPhase('release');\n440\t      return;\n441\t    }\n442\t\n443\t    // Brief polling window for card-based processing\n444\t    const timer = setTimeout(() => setPhase('release'), holdDurationMs);\n445\t    return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n446\t  }, [holdDurationMs]);\n447\t\n448\t  // ... render based on phase\n449\t}\n450\t```\n451\t\n452\t**What happens when the user closes the browser:**\n453\t\n454\t```mermaid\n455\tflowchart TD\n456\t    A[User closes browser during 'processing'] --> B[Order exists in DB with status: payment_pending]\n457\t    B --> C{Stripe webhook arrives later}\n458\t    C -->|payment_intent.succeeded| D[Update order to payment_confirmed]\n459\t    C -->|payment_intent.payment_failed| E[Update order to payment_failed]\n460\t    D --> F[Send success email to user]\n461\t    E --> G[Send failure email with retry link]\n462\t    F --> H[Next time user visits site]\n463\t    G --> H\n464\t    H --> I[Check for pending orders in session/DB]\n465\t    I --> J[Show banner: 'Your payment for Order #12345 was confirmed!']\n466\t```\n467\t\n468\t**Email notification contract:**\n469\t- On entering `processing`: send \"Payment received\" email with order number and timeline\n470\t- On `payment_intent.succeeded` webhook: send \"Payment confirmed\" email with receipt\n471\t- On `payment_intent.payment_failed`: send \"Payment failed\" email with a link to retry (`/orders/:id/retry-payment`)\n472\t\n473\t**Tradeoffs:** Creating the order immediately in `payment_pending` state means your inventory system must handle orders that may never convert (payment fails). Soft-reserve inventory during `payment_pending` with a TTL (7 days for bank transfers) and release it back if payment ultimately fails. Alternatively, use a two-stage model: \"payment pending\" creates a reservation but not an order, and the full order is created only on `succeeded`. The first approach is simpler and gives users a better experience (immediate order number); the second is safer for inventory-constrained businesses.\n474\t\n475\t---\n476\t\n477\t### Q6. Re-surfacing payment outcome after user navigates away\n478\t\n479\t**Problem framing:** The most common real-world scenario: user submits payment, browser shows \"processing,\" user opens a new tab to check email, then never returns to the checkout tab. The webhook arrives at your server 5 minutes later. How does the user learn the outcome?\n480\t\n481\t**Approach:**\n482\t\n483\tThis requires a persistence layer on the server that outlives the browser session, and a check-on-visit mechanism that surfaces the result next time the user engages with your site.\n484\t\n485\t**Step 1: Persist the pending payment state server-side at checkout initiation**\n486\t\n487\t```javascript\n488\t// When creating PaymentIntent — store the pending state immediately\n489\tasync function createPaymentIntent(userId, cartId, amount) {\n490\t  const pi = await stripe.paymentIntents.create({\n491\t    amount,\n492\t    currency: 'usd',\n493\t    metadata: { userId, cartId, orderId: order.id },\n494\t  });\n495\t\n496\t  // Store in DB immediately — before user confirms\n497\t  await db.pendingPayments.upsert({\n498\t    userId,\n499\t    paymentIntentId: pi.id,\n500\t    orderId: order.id,\n501\t    status: 'created',\n502\t    createdAt: new Date(),\n503\t  });\n504\t\n505\t  return pi;\n506\t}\n507\t```\n508\t\n509\t**Step 2: Webhook handler updates the persistent record**\n510\t\n511\t```javascript\n512\t// payment_intent.succeeded webhook\n513\tawait db.pendingPayments.update(\n514\t  { paymentIntentId: pi.id },\n515\t  {\n516\t    status: 'succeeded',\n517\t    resolvedAt: new Date(),\n518\t    notified: false, // Flag: user hasn't seen this yet\n519\t  }\n520\t);\n521\t\n522\t// payment_intent.payment_failed webhook\n523\tawait db.pendingPayments.update(\n524\t  { paymentIntentId: pi.id },\n525\t  {\n526\t    status: 'failed',\n527\t    failureCode: event.data.object.last_payment_error?.code,\n528\t    resolvedAt: new Date(),\n529\t    notified: false,\n530\t  }\n531\t);\n532\t```\n533\t\n534\t**Step 3: Check for unnotified outcomes on next page load / login**\n535\t\n536\t```javascript\n537\t// Middleware or layout component — runs on every page load for logged-in users\n538\tasync function checkPendingPaymentNotifications(userId) {\n539\t  const pending = await db.pendingPayments.findFirst({\n540\t    where: {\n541\t      userId,\n542\t      status: { in: ['succeeded', 'failed'] },\n543\t      notified: false,\n544\t    },\n545\t    orderBy: { resolvedAt: 'desc' },\n546\t  });\n547\t\n548\t  return pending;\n549\t}\n550\t```\n551\t\n552\t**Step 4: Surface as a banner or modal on next visit**\n553\t\n554\t```javascript\n555\t// In your app's layout/root component\n556\tconst pendingNotification = await checkPendingPaymentNotifications(userId);\n557\t\n558\tif (pendingNotification?.status === 'succeeded') {\n559\t  showBanner({\n560\t    type: 'success',\n561\t    message: `Your payment for Order #${pendingNotification.orderId} was confirmed!`,\n562\t    action: { label: 'View Order', href: `/orders/${pendingNotification.orderId}` },\n563\t    onDismiss: () => markNotified(pendingNotification.id),\n564\t  });\n565\t}\n566\t\n567\tif (pendingNotification?.status === 'failed') {\n568\t  showBanner({\n569\t    type: 'error',\n570\t    message: `Your payment for Order #${pendingNotification.orderId} failed.`,\n571\t    action: { label: 'Retry Payment', href: `/orders/${pendingNotification.orderId}/retry` },\n572\t    onDismiss: () => markNotified(pendingNotification.id),\n573\t  });\n574\t}\n575\t```\n576\t\n577\t**Guest users (no login):** Store the PaymentIntent ID in a long-lived cookie or `localStorage` at checkout initiation:\n578\t\n579\t```javascript\n580\tlocalStorage.setItem('pending_payment', JSON.stringify({\n581\t  paymentIntentId: pi.id,\n582\t  orderId: order.id,\n583\t  expiresAt: Date.now() + (7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000), // 7 days\n584\t}));\n585\t```\n586\t\n587\tOn next visit, check `localStorage`, call `/api/payment/status/:piId`, and show the appropriate banner. Clean up after display.\n588\t\n589\t**Tradeoffs:** The server-side persistence approach requires a `pendingPayments` table and a per-user query on every page load — low cost but non-zero. Cache the \"has pending notifications\" check in a Redis boolean keyed to userId, invalidated by the webhook handler. The localStorage approach for guests works but has edge cases: different device, cleared storage, or 7-day TTL expiry. Email is the most reliable fallback for guests — always send a confirmation or failure email regardless of frontend notification strategy.\n590\t\n591\t---\n592\t\n593\t### Q7. Reconciling a \"failed\" UI with a later-arriving `payment_intent.succeeded` webhook\n594\t\n595\t**Problem framing:** A common failure mode: the user's `confirmCardPayment` call times out at the network layer (the HTTP request hangs for >30 seconds and the browser gives up), the frontend shows an error, and the user sees a \"Payment failed\" screen. Meanwhile, Stripe did receive the request and the payment succeeds. The webhook arrives at your server, your server marks it succeeded, but the user is currently staring at an error message. This is a costly false negative — the user may try to pay again, creating a duplicate charge attempt, or abandon entirely.\n596\t\n597\t**Approach:**\n598\t\n599\t```mermaid\n600\tsequenceDiagram\n601\t    participant Browser\n602\t    participant YourAPI as Your Backend\n603\t    participant Stripe\n604\t\n605\t    Browser->>Stripe: stripe.confirmCardPayment() [TIMES OUT]\n606\t    Note over Browser: Shows \"Payment failed\" error\n607\t    Stripe->>YourAPI: payment_intent.succeeded webhook\n608\t    YourAPI->>YourAPI: Updates DB: pi.status = succeeded\n609\t    YourAPI->>YourAPI: Publishes to pub/sub: payment:pi_xxx:succeeded\n610\t    Note over Browser: If SSE connection still open:\n611\t    YourAPI-->>Browser: SSE event: payment.succeeded\n612\t    Browser->>Browser: Overrides error state, shows success\n613\t```\n614\t\n615\t**The key architectural requirement:** Your SSE/WebSocket connection must remain open even after the frontend shows an error — at least for a grace period.\n616\t\n617\t**Implementation:**\n618\t\n619\t```javascript\n620\tfunction PaymentConfirmationPage({ paymentIntentId }) {\n621\t  const [uiStatus, setUiStatus] = useState('confirming');\n622\t  const [webhookStatus, setWebhookStatus] = useState(null);\n623\t\n624\t  // The SSE subscription runs independently of the confirmCardPayment outcome\n625\t  useEffect(() => {\n626\t    const source = new EventSource(`/api/payment-status/stream?pi=${paymentIntentId}`);\n627\t\n628\t    source.addEventListener('payment.succeeded', () => {\n629\t      // Override whatever the UI currently shows\n630\t      setWebhookStatus('succeeded');\n631\t    });\n632\t\n633\t    source.addEventListener('payment.failed', (event) => {\n634\t      setWebhookStatus('failed');\n635\t    });\n636\t\n637\t    // Keep SSE open for 5 minutes regardless of confirmCardPayment outcome\n638\t    const timeout = setTimeout(() => source.close(), 5 * 60 * 1000);\n639\t    return () => { source.close(); clearTimeout(timeout); };\n640\t  }, [paymentIntentId]);\n641\t\n642\t  // confirmCardPayment result (may time out or fail transiently)\n643\t  useEffect(() => {\n644\t    stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, { payment_method: { card: cardElement } })\n645\t      .then(({ paymentIntent, error }) => {\n646\t        if (error?.type === 'api_connection_error' || error?.type === 'api_error') {\n647\t          // Transient — do NOT show permanent failure\n648\t          // SSE will correct the state when webhook arrives\n649\t          setUiStatus('network_error_waiting');\n650\t        } else if (error) {\n651\t          setUiStatus('failed');\n652\t        } else if (paymentIntent.status === 'succeeded') {\n653\t          setUiStatus('awaiting_server_confirm');\n654\t        }\n655\t      });\n656\t  }, []);\n657\t\n658\t  // Webhook status overrides confirmCardPayment status\n659\t  const displayStatus = webhookStatus ?? uiStatus;\n660\t\n661\t  // Render based on displayStatus\n662\t}\n663\t```\n664\t\n665\t**When the tab is closed (no SSE connection):** The webhook arrives, server processes it, stores `status: succeeded` in DB. Next time the user visits (via the pending notification banner from Q6), they see the correct success state. Additionally, your fulfillment system processes the order — so even if the user never comes back, the order is fulfilled.\n666\t\n667\t**Showing the correction to the user:** If the SSE delivers `payment.succeeded` while the screen shows a failure message:\n668\t\n669\t```javascript\n670\tif (webhookStatus === 'succeeded' && uiStatus === 'failed') {\n671\t  // Don't silently swap to success — this is jarring\n672\t  // Show an explanatory transition\n673\t  showModal({\n674\t    title: \"Good news — your payment went through!\",\n675\t    body: \"We had a network hiccup earlier, but your payment was successfully processed.\",\n676\t    action: { label: \"View Order\", href: `/orders/${orderId}` }\n677\t  });\n678\t}\n679\t```\n680\t\n681\t**Tradeoffs:** The 5-minute SSE grace period uses server resources (open connection) even for error states. This is a reasonable tradeoff: the cost per connection is very low (one file descriptor, minimal memory) and the benefit (catching the timeout-then-success scenario) is high. Alternatively, on network error, immediately start polling your server-side status endpoint instead of keeping SSE open — polling has higher latency but doesn't require a persistent connection.\n682\t\n683\t---\n684\t\n685\t## Deep Questions\n686\t\n687\t### Q8. Real-time notification system across all frontend contexts\n688\t\n689\t**Problem framing:** A payment notification system must work correctly across three fundamentally different contexts that have radically different capabilities: (a) an active tab with a live SSE connection that can receive push events; (b) a tab that was closed and re-opened within a short window; and (c) a mobile app that has backgrounded — JavaScript is suspended, but the user's device can receive push notifications. Each context requires a different delivery mechanism, but all three must show consistent data with no duplicates.\n690\t\n691\t**Approach:**\n692\t\n693\tFull architecture from Stripe webhook to each frontend context:\n694\t\n695\t```mermaid\n696\tflowchart TD\n697\t    W[Stripe Webhook] --> V[Signature Verification]\n698\t    V --> D[Write to DB: payments table]\n699\t    D --> P[Publish to Pub/Sub: payment:pi_xxx]\n700\t    P --> S1[SSE Hub: active connections]\n701\t    P --> S2[Push Notification Service: FCM/APNs]\n702\t    S1 --> C1[Active checkout tab]\n703\t    D --> C2[DB poll on tab reopen]\n704\t    S2 --> C3[Mobile background notification]\n705\t```\n706\t\n707\t**Case A: Active checkout tab with SSE**\n708\t\n709\tThe SSE connection is established when the payment confirmation page loads, keyed to the `paymentIntentId`. The connection is registered in an in-memory map (or Redis if horizontally scaled):\n710\t\n711\t```javascript\n712\t// Server: in-memory connection registry (use Redis for multi-instance)\n713\tconst sseConnections = new Map(); // paymentIntentId → Set<Response>\n714\t\n715\t// Register on SSE connect\n716\tapp.get('/api/payment-status/stream', (req, res) => {\n717\t  const { pi } = req.query;\n718\t\n719\t  // SSE headers\n720\t  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream', 'Cache-Control': 'no-cache' });\n721\t\n722\t  if (!sseConnections.has(pi)) sseConnections.set(pi, new Set());\n723\t  sseConnections.get(pi).add(res);\n724\t\n725\t  req.on('close', () => {\n726\t    sseConnections.get(pi)?.delete(res);\n727\t  });\n728\t});\n729\t\n730\t// Called by webhook handler after writing to DB\n731\tfunction pushToActiveTabs(paymentIntentId, event) {\n732\t  const connections = sseConnections.get(paymentIntentId);\n733\t  if (!connections?.size) return;\n734\t\n735\t  const payload = `event: ${event.type}\\ndata: ${JSON.stringify(event.data)}\\nid: ${event.id}\\n\\n`;\n736\t  for (const res of connections) {\n737\t    res.write(payload);\n738\t    if (event.type.endsWith('.succeeded') || event.type.endsWith('.failed')) {\n739\t      res.end(); // Close connection after terminal event\n740\t    }\n741\t  }\n742\t}\n743\t```\n744\t\n745\t**Event deduplication:** Each SSE event carries a unique `id` (the DB event ID). The browser's `EventSource` API tracks the last received `id` and sends it as `Last-Event-ID` on reconnect. Your server resends any events with ID greater than `Last-Event-ID`:\n746\t\n747\t```javascript\n748\tapp.get('/api/payment-status/stream', (req, res) => {\n749\t  const lastEventId = req.headers['last-event-id'];\n750\t  const { pi } = req.query;\n751\t\n752\t  // Replay missed events on reconnect\n753\t  if (lastEventId) {\n754\t    const missed = await db.paymentEvents.findMany({\n755\t      where: { paymentIntentId: pi, id: { gt: parseInt(lastEventId) } },\n756\t    });\n757\t    for (const event of missed) {\n758\t      res.write(`id: ${event.id}\\nevent: ${event.type}\\ndata: ${JSON.stringify(event.data)}\\n\\n`);\n759\t    }\n760\t  }\n761\t\n762\t  // Continue with live subscription...\n763\t});\n764\t```\n765\t\n766\t**Case B: Tab closed, re-opened within 10 minutes**\n767\t\n768\tOn mount of the confirmation page (or any page that should check payment status), check the server directly:\n769\t\n770\t```javascript\n771\tasync function checkPaymentStatusOnMount(paymentIntentId) {\n772\t  // Simple REST call — no SSE needed yet\n773\t  const response = await fetch(`/api/payment/status/${paymentIntentId}`);\n774\t  const { status, orderId, resolvedAt } = await response.json();\n775\t\n776\t  if (status === 'succeeded' || status === 'failed') {\n777\t    // Already resolved during the tab's absence\n778\t    return { status, orderId, source: 'db_poll' };\n779\t  }\n780\t\n781\t  // Still processing — establish SSE for live updates\n782\t  return { status: 'processing', source: 'db_poll' };\n783\t}\n784\t```\n785\t\n786\tThe key: this REST call reads from **your DB** (populated by the webhook), not from Stripe. Sub-millisecond response, no Stripe API rate limit consumption.\n787\t\n788\t**Case C: Mobile app backgrounded**\n789\t\n790\tWhen JavaScript is suspended (iOS background, Android doze mode), SSE connections are severed and polling is impossible. Use push notifications:\n791\t\n792\t```javascript\n793\t// At payment initiation, register device token\n794\tasync function initPaymentWithPushNotification(paymentIntentId, deviceToken) {\n795\t  await db.paymentPushRegistrations.upsert({\n796\t    paymentIntentId,\n797\t    deviceToken,\n798\t    platform: getPlatform(), // 'ios' | 'android' | 'web'\n799\t  });\n800\t}\n801\t\n802\t// Webhook handler sends push notification\n803\tasync function notifyMobileBackground(paymentIntentId, event) {\n804\t  const registrations = await db.paymentPushRegistrations.findMany({\n805\t    where: { paymentIntentId },\n806\t  });\n807\t\n808\t  for (const reg of registrations) {\n809\t    if (reg.platform === 'ios') {\n810\t      await apns.send(reg.deviceToken, {\n811\t        alert: event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded'\n812\t          ? 'Payment confirmed! Your order is being prepared.'\n813\t          : 'Payment issue — tap to retry.',\n814\t        badge: 1,\n815\t        payload: { paymentIntentId, orderId: event.data.object.metadata.orderId },\n816\t      });\n817\t    } else if (reg.platform === 'android') {\n818\t      await fcm.send(reg.deviceToken, {\n819\t        notification: { title: 'Payment Update', body: '...' },\n820\t        data: { paymentIntentId },\n821\t      });\n822\t    }\n823\t  }\n824\t}\n825\t```\n826\t\n827\t**Handling reconnection and missed events — unified sequence:**\n828\t\n829\t```mermaid\n830\tsequenceDiagram\n831\t    participant Browser\n832\t    participant SSEServer as SSE Server\n833\t    participant DB\n834\t\n835\t    Browser->>SSEServer: GET /stream?pi=pi_xxx (Last-Event-ID: 42)\n836\t    SSEServer->>DB: SELECT * FROM events WHERE pi_id=xxx AND id > 42\n837\t    DB-->>SSEServer: [event id=43: payment.succeeded]\n838\t    SSEServer-->>Browser: id: 43\\nevent: payment.succeeded\\ndata: {...}\n839\t    Note over Browser: Replays missed event, shows success\n840\t```\n841\t\n842\t**Tradeoffs:** The in-memory `sseConnections` map works on a single server instance but fails in a horizontally-scaled deployment (multiple API servers). Replace with Redis pub/sub: each server instance subscribes to the payment's Redis channel; the webhook handler publishes there; all instances receive it and push to their own local SSE connections. This is the standard pattern for scaling SSE.\n843\t\n844\t---\n845\t\n846\t### Q9. Webhook → frontend pipeline: consistency and no stale states\n847\t\n848\t**Problem framing:** The pipeline from \"Stripe sends webhook\" to \"user sees status update\" has multiple steps, each of which can fail, produce a partial write, or arrive out of order. If you push to the frontend before writing to the DB, the UI shows `succeeded` but a database crash leaves the DB in `processing` — permanently inconsistent. If you push after the DB write but before order fulfillment, the user sees success but the order isn't fulfilled. This section is about ensuring the DB state and the frontend state are always consistent.\n849\t\n850\t**Approach:**\n851\t\n852\tThe full pipeline with consistency guarantees at each step:\n853\t\n854\t```mermaid\n855\tsequenceDiagram\n856\t    participant Stripe\n857\t    participant WebhookReceiver as Webhook Receiver\n858\t    participant DB\n859\t    participant PubSub\n860\t    participant SSEServer as SSE Server\n861\t    participant Browser\n862\t\n863\t    Stripe->>WebhookReceiver: POST /webhooks/stripe\n864\t    WebhookReceiver->>WebhookReceiver: Verify Stripe-Signature\n865\t    Note over WebhookReceiver: STOP if signature invalid → 400\n866\t    WebhookReceiver->>DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION\n867\t    WebhookReceiver->>DB: Upsert payment record (idempotency: event_id)\n868\t    WebhookReceiver->>DB: Create order fulfillment record\n869\t    WebhookReceiver->>DB: COMMIT TRANSACTION\n870\t    Note over WebhookReceiver: Only push AFTER successful commit\n871\t    WebhookReceiver->>PubSub: Publish payment:pi_xxx event\n872\t    WebhookReceiver-->>Stripe: 200 OK (fast — before push reaches browser)\n873\t    PubSub->>SSEServer: Event notification\n874\t    SSEServer->>DB: SELECT current_status WHERE pi_id=xxx\n875\t    Note over SSEServer: Read from DB, not from event payload\n876\t    SSEServer-->>Browser: SSE event: payment.succeeded\n877\t```\n878\t\n879\t**Step 1: Signature verification**\n880\t\n881\t```javascript\n882\tapp.post('/webhooks/stripe', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), async (req, res) => {\n883\t  const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\n884\t\n885\t  let event;\n886\t  try {\n887\t    // constructEvent requires raw body — never parse with JSON middleware first\n888\t    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET);\n889\t  } catch (err) {\n890\t    console.error('Webhook signature failed:', err.message);\n891\t    return res.status(400).send('Invalid signature');\n892\t  }\n893\t\n894\t  // Event is now cryptographically verified — cannot be forged\n895\t  await handleVerifiedEvent(event);\n896\t  res.json({ received: true }); // Must respond quickly — Stripe has a 30s timeout\n897\t});\n898\t```\n899\t\n900\t**Step 2: Idempotent DB write inside a transaction**\n901\t\n902\t```javascript\n903\tasync function handleVerifiedEvent(event) {\n904\t  await db.transaction(async (tx) => {\n905\t    // Idempotency check — Stripe may deliver the same event multiple times\n906\t    const existing = await tx.processedWebhookEvents.findUnique({\n907\t      where: { stripeEventId: event.id },\n908\t    });\n909\t    if (existing) return; // Already processed — skip\n910\t\n911\t    // Write event record first (for idempotency)\n912\t    await tx.processedWebhookEvents.create({\n913\t      data: { stripeEventId: event.id, type: event.type, processedAt: new Date() },\n914\t    });\n915\t\n916\t    // Update payment status\n917\t    await tx.payments.update({\n918\t      where: { stripePaymentIntentId: event.data.object.id },\n919\t      data: {\n920\t        status: event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded' ? 'succeeded' : 'failed',\n921\t        resolvedAt: new Date(),\n922\t      },\n923\t    });\n924\t\n925\t    // Trigger fulfillment (within same transaction for atomicity)\n926\t    if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {\n927\t      await tx.orders.update({\n928\t        where: { paymentIntentId: event.data.object.id },\n929\t        data: { status: 'confirmed', fulfilledAt: new Date() },\n930\t      });\n931\t    }\n932\t  });\n933\t\n934\t  // ONLY after successful commit — push to pub/sub\n935\t  await pubSub.publish(`payment:${event.data.object.id}`, {\n936\t    type: event.type,\n937\t    paymentIntentId: event.data.object.id,\n938\t  });\n939\t}\n940\t```\n941\t\n942\t**Step 3: SSE server reads from DB before pushing to browser**\n943\t\n944\tThis is the critical consistency guarantee — the SSE server does NOT push the event payload directly to the browser. It reads fresh from the DB:\n945\t\n946\t```javascript\n947\tpubSub.subscribe(`payment:${paymentIntentId}`, async (pubSubEvent) => {\n948\t  // Re-read from DB — not from the pub/sub payload\n949\t  const payment = await db.payments.findUnique({\n950\t    where: { stripePaymentIntentId: paymentIntentId },\n951\t    select: { status: true, orderId: true, resolvedAt: true },\n952\t  });\n953\t\n954\t  // Push what the DB actually says — never a stale intermediate state\n955\t  const ssePayload = {\n956\t    type: `payment.${payment.status}`,\n957\t    data: { status: payment.status, orderId: payment.orderId },\n958\t    id: payment.eventId, // DB-generated sequential ID for Last-Event-ID\n959\t  };\n960\t\n961\t  pushToActiveTabs(paymentIntentId, ssePayload);\n962\t});\n963\t```\n964\t\n965\t**Why DB-read-before-push prevents stale state:**\n966\t- Pub/sub messages can arrive out of order (Kafka/Redis can reorder messages under load)\n967\t- If a `payment_intent.payment_failed` message arrives before `payment_intent.succeeded` (race in your webhook processing), pushing the pub/sub payload directly could show \"failed\" to the user even though the DB has `succeeded`\n968\t- Reading from DB always shows the current committed state — the most recent write wins by DB constraints\n969\t\n970\t**Stripe returns 200 immediately vs. fulfillment time:** Return `200 OK` to Stripe as soon as signature verification succeeds. Don't wait for the transaction to commit. This prevents Stripe from retrying the webhook (which has a 30-second timeout). Process asynchronously:\n971\t\n972\t```javascript\n973\tapp.post('/webhooks/stripe', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), async (req, res) => {\n974\t  // Verify synchronously\n975\t  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, webhookSecret);\n976\t\n977\t  // Respond immediately\n978\t  res.json({ received: true });\n979\t\n980\t  // Process asynchronously — outside the request handler\n981\t  setImmediate(() => handleVerifiedEvent(event));\n982\t});\n983\t```\n984\t\n985\tBetter: push to a queue (SQS, BullMQ) and process with a worker. This decouples Stripe's 30-second timeout from your processing time.\n986\t\n987\t**Tradeoffs:** The DB-read-before-push adds one extra DB read per push event. This is almost always acceptable — a single indexed read is microseconds. The alternative (push the event payload directly) is faster but opens the consistency gap. The transaction-per-event approach serializes webhook processing for the same payment intent — if two events arrive simultaneously, the second transaction's idempotency check serializes behind the first, preventing race conditions.\n988\t\n989\t---\n990\t\n991\t### Q10. POS dashboard: concurrent payments, normalized store, render optimization\n992\t\n993\t**Problem framing:** A restaurant POS processing 20 simultaneous orders has different requirements than a single-user checkout: state is shared across multiple payments, events arrive continuously and potentially out of order, the UI must update individual order cards without re-rendering unrelated ones, and the WebSocket must handle reconnection without losing events. The standard \"array of payments in React state\" approach breaks down because any status update causes a full list re-render.\n994\t\n995\t**Approach:**\n996\t\n997\t**WebSocket message schema:**\n998\t\n999\tDesign for idempotency, ordering, and partial updates:\n1000\t\n1001\t```typescript\n1002\tinterface PaymentStatusEvent {\n1003\t  // Routing\n1004\t  eventId: string;          // Globally unique — for deduplication\n1005\t  orderId: string;          // Which order this applies to\n1006\t  paymentIntentId: string;\n1007\t\n1008\t  // Ordering (handle out-of-order delivery)\n1009\t  sequenceNumber: number;   // Monotonically increasing per orderId\n1010\t  timestamp: number;        // Unix ms — for display only, not ordering\n1011\t\n1012\t  // Payload\n1013\t  type:\n1014\t    | 'payment.created'\n1015\t    | 'payment.processing'\n1016\t    | 'payment.succeeded'\n1017\t    | 'payment.failed'\n1018\t    | 'payment.refunded';\n1019\t\n1020\t  // Partial update — only include changed fields\n1021\t  patch: {\n1022\t    status?: OrderStatus;\n1023\t    amount?: number;\n1024\t    paymentMethod?: string;\n1025\t    failureCode?: string;\n1026\t    receiptUrl?: string;\n1027\t  };\n1028\t}\n1029\t```\n1030\t\n1031\tThe `patch` design (sending only changed fields) reduces message size and simplifies the reducer:\n1032\t\n1033\t```typescript\n1034\t// Server sends partial updates, not full order objects\n1035\tws.send(JSON.stringify({\n1036\t  eventId: 'evt_xxx',\n1037\t  orderId: 'ord_123',\n1038\t  sequenceNumber: 7,\n1039\t  type: 'payment.succeeded',\n1040\t  patch: { status: 'paid', receiptUrl: 'https://...' },\n1041\t  // NOT the entire order with 30 fields\n1042\t}));\n1043\t```\n1044\t\n1045\t**Client-side normalized store:**\n1046\t\n1047\tUse a `Map<orderId, Order>` instead of an array. Map updates are O(1) and don't require array scans:\n1048\t\n1049\t```typescript\n1050\t// Zustand store with normalized Map\n1051\tinterface POSStore {\n1052\t  orders: Map<string, Order>;\n1053\t  processedEventIds: Set<string>; // For deduplication\n1054\t  lastSequenceNumbers: Map<string, number>; // For out-of-order handling\n1055\t  applyEvent: (event: PaymentStatusEvent) => void;\n1056\t}\n1057\t\n1058\tconst usePOSStore = create<POSStore>((set, get) => ({\n1059\t  orders: new Map(),\n1060\t  processedEventIds: new Set(),\n1061\t  lastSequenceNumbers: new Map(),\n1062\t\n1063\t  applyEvent: (event) => set((state) => {\n1064\t    // 1. Deduplication\n1065\t    if (state.processedEventIds.has(event.eventId)) {\n1066\t      return state; // Already applied — ignore\n1067\t    }\n1068\t\n1069\t    // 2. Out-of-order detection\n1070\t    const lastSeq = state.lastSequenceNumbers.get(event.orderId) ?? -1;\n1071\t    if (event.sequenceNumber <= lastSeq) {\n1072\t      // Stale event — ignore (we have a newer state for this order)\n1073\t      return state;\n1074\t    }\n1075\t\n1076\t    // 3. Apply patch to existing order\n1077\t    const existingOrder = state.orders.get(event.orderId);\n1078\t    if (!existingOrder) return state; // Order not in our store yet\n1079\t\n1080\t    const updatedOrder = { ...existingOrder, ...event.patch };\n1081\t\n1082\t    // 4. Immutable update — new Map to trigger React re-render\n1083\t    const newOrders = new Map(state.orders);\n1084\t    newOrders.set(event.orderId, updatedOrder);\n1085\t\n1086\t    const newSeqNumbers = new Map(state.lastSequenceNumbers);\n1087\t    newSeqNumbers.set(event.orderId, event.sequenceNumber);\n1088\t\n1089\t    const newEventIds = new Set(state.processedEventIds);\n1090\t    newEventIds.add(event.eventId);\n1091\t    // Prune old event IDs to prevent unbounded growth\n1092\t    if (newEventIds.size > 10_000) {\n1093\t      const [firstId] = newEventIds;\n1094\t      newEventIds.delete(firstId);\n1095\t    }\n1096\t\n1097\t    return {\n1098\t      orders: newOrders,\n1099\t      processedEventIds: newEventIds,\n1100\t      lastSequenceNumbers: newSeqNumbers,\n1101\t    };\n1102\t  }),\n1103\t}));\n1104\t```\n1105\t\n1106\t**Render optimization — React.memo + selector per order:**\n1107\t\n1108\tThe critical insight: each order card should subscribe only to its own order's state, not the entire `orders` Map. Use selectors:\n1109\t\n1110\t```typescript\n1111\t// Each OrderCard subscribes to only its own order\n1112\tconst OrderCard = React.memo(({ orderId }: { orderId: string }) => {\n1113\t  // Selector: extracts only this order from the store\n1114\t  const order = usePOSStore(\n1115\t    useCallback((state) => state.orders.get(orderId), [orderId]),\n1116\t    // Equality: only re-render if this specific order changed\n1117\t    (a, b) => a?.status === b?.status && a?.amount === b?.amount\n1118\t  );\n1119\t\n1120\t  if (!order) return null;\n1121\t\n1122\t  return (\n1123\t    <div className={`order-card order-card--${order.status}`}>\n1124\t      <span className=\"order-number\">#{order.number}</span>\n1125\t      <span className=\"order-amount\">${(order.amount / 100).toFixed(2)}</span>\n1126\t      <PaymentStatusBadge status={order.status} />\n1127\t    </div>\n1128\t  );\n1129\t});\n1130\t```\n1131\t\n1132\t**Order list — virtualization for 20+ orders:**\n1133\t\n1134\tEven with memoized order cards, rendering 20+ cards simultaneously can cause jank if each card is complex. Use `react-window` or `@tanstack/virtual` for the order list:\n1135\t\n1136\t```typescript\n1137\timport { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\n1138\t\n1139\tfunction OrderList() {\n1140\t  const orderIds = usePOSStore(\n1141\t    (state) => Array.from(state.orders.keys()),\n1142\t    // Shallow compare arrays — only re-render if the list of IDs changes\n1143\t    (a, b) => a.length === b.length && a.every((id, i) => id === b[i])\n1144\t  );\n1145\t\n1146\t  return (\n1147\t    <List\n1148\t      height={600}\n1149\t      itemCount={orderIds.length}\n1150\t      itemSize={120} // px per order card\n1151\t      width=\"100%\"\n1152\t    >\n1153\t      {({ index, style }) => (\n1154\t        <div style={style}>\n1155\t          <OrderCard key={orderIds[index]} orderId={orderIds[index]} />\n1156\t        </div>\n1157\t      )}\n1158\t    </List>\n1159\t  );\n1160\t}\n1161\t```\n1162\t\n1163\t**WebSocket reconnection with event replay:**\n1164\t\n1165\t```typescript\n1166\tclass POSWebSocket {\n1167\t  private ws: WebSocket | null = null;\n1168\t  private lastEventId: string | null = null;\n1169\t  private reconnectDelay = 1000;\n1170\t  private store: POSStore;\n1171\t\n1172\t  connect() {\n1173\t    const url = `/api/pos/ws?lastEventId=${this.lastEventId ?? ''}`;\n1174\t    this.ws = new WebSocket(url);\n1175\t\n1176\t    this.ws.onmessage = (e) => {\n1177\t      const event: PaymentStatusEvent = JSON.parse(e.data);\n1178\t      this.lastEventId = event.eventId;\n1179\t      this.store.applyEvent(event); // Deduplication handles replayed events\n1180\t    };\n1181\t\n1182\t    this.ws.onclose = () => {\n1183\t      // Exponential backoff with cap\n1184\t      setTimeout(() => {\n1185\t        this.reconnectDelay = Math.min(this.reconnectDelay * 2, 30_000);\n1186\t        this.connect();\n1187\t      }, this.reconnectDelay);\n1188\t    };\n1189\t\n1190\t    this.ws.onopen = () => {\n1191\t      this.reconnectDelay = 1000; // Reset on successful connect\n1192\t    };\n1193\t  }\n1194\t}\n1195\t```\n1196\t\n1197\t**Server replays events on reconnect:**\n1198\t\n1199\t```javascript\n1200\tws.on('connection', async (socket, req) => {\n1201\t  const { lastEventId } = req.query;\n1202\t\n1203\t  // Replay missed events since lastEventId\n1204\t  if (lastEventId) {\n1205\t    const missed = await db.posEvents.findMany({\n1206\t      where: { id: { gt: lastEventId } },\n1207\t      orderBy: { sequenceNumber: 'asc' },\n1208\t    });\n1209\t    for (const event of missed) {\n1210\t      socket.send(JSON.stringify(event));\n1211\t    }\n1212\t  }\n1213\t\n1214\t  // Subscribe to new events\n1215\t  pubSub.subscribe('pos:events', (event) => {\n1216\t    socket.send(JSON.stringify(event));\n1217\t  });\n1218\t});\n1219\t```\n1220\t\n1221\t**Out-of-order handling — concrete example:**\n1222\t\n1223\t```\n1224\tEvents arrive:  [seq:7, ord:123, succeeded] then [seq:5, ord:123, processing]\n1225\t                 ↓                                  ↓\n1226\tStore sees:      Apply seq:7 (lastSeq → 7)         Reject seq:5 (5 ≤ 7, stale)\n1227\tResult:          Order 123 shows \"succeeded\"        No change — correct!\n1228\t```\n1229\t\n1230\tWithout sequence numbers, the `processing` event arriving late would incorrectly revert a `succeeded` order to `processing`.\n1231\t\n1232\t**Tradeoffs:** The normalized `Map` over an array is essential for O(1) updates but requires mutable-style updates wrapped in immutable patterns (creating a new Map on every update). For 20 orders this is fine; for 10,000 orders, consider a more efficient immutable data structure (Immer, structural sharing). The `processedEventIds` Set grows unboundedly — prune it on a sliding window (keep last N event IDs, where N > your maximum expected duplicate window). The sequence-number approach assumes events are ordered per `orderId` — if your Kafka/queue doesn't partition by orderId, sequence numbers may not be reliable; use timestamps as a secondary sort key in that case, accepting that two events in the same millisecond are treated as concurrent and either ordering is acceptable.\n1233\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01XjZ9HT3i2UMuwALeh5JgXq","session_id":"32aa5972-b92a-4580-b5c5-f0297ce83ccb","uuid":"c6d07311-7452-4baf-872b-7f95ff48f19c","timestamp":"2026-06-20T19:05:09.851Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write answers for payments-system section 05: Performance & Core Web Vitals"}
